---
/**
 * The bloom field - the atmosphere behind every act.
 *
 * Direction chosen by the owner at 8.1's Gate A (gate-log.md): overlapping CSS radial
 * gradients, drifting. Picked over a painted static raster and over a turbulence-masked
 * variant, both built and measured at `/blooms` before the gate.
 *
 * Reference: superlist.com's blurry-light background (`reference-study.md` §3). What we
 * take is the *idea* - soft irregular colour fields behind content, changing temperature
 * by section, feathered into the ground rather than edged. What we do differently is
 * measured and deliberate: theirs is a single 321 KB PNG that never moves and never
 * changes hue, ours is zero bytes, drifts, and takes every colour from `tokens.css`, so a
 * new act is one attribute rather than a new asset.
 *
 * **Rebuilt at 8.2's Gate C rev 7 (owner): the field is three independent orbs, not one
 * painted plate.** It was a single element carrying three baked-in gradient stops, which
 * meant the only motion available to it was moving all three together - the owner's read
 * was that this was too subtle to register and too plain to feel modern. Each light now
 * orbits on its own period and direction, so they converge, overlap and separate without
 * ever repeating the same arrangement; `mix-blend-mode: screen` is what makes an overlap
 * *merge* into a brighter colour rather than one blob occluding another.
 *
 * **`--bloom-scroll`** (0–1) rotates and swells the whole field, so the atmosphere answers
 * the visitor's scrolling on top of its own idle life. A section that wants that sets the
 * property; anything that does not gets the idle field and nothing else.
 *
 * Drift and scroll are both composited `transform`s and the merge is `mix-blend-mode`, so
 * nothing here touches layout or paint - which is exactly why `design-system.md` §6
 * forbids animating `filter`, `width` or `box-shadow` in anything scrubbed. Cost is
 * re-measured under 6x CPU throttle whenever this file changes.
 */

/** Each act owns a temperature, so the page changes as you scroll (design-system.md §2). */
export type Act = 'hero' | 'problem' | 'feed' | 'notify' | 'cta';

interface Props {
  /** Which act's palette to wear. */
  readonly act: Act;
  /**
   * How present the field is. `ambient` sits behind copy; `feature` is the strongest
   * bloom moment on the page and is reserved for the hero and the final CTA.
   */
  readonly intensity?: 'ambient' | 'feature';
  readonly class?: string | undefined;
}

const { act, intensity = 'ambient', class: className } = Astro.props;
---

<div
  class:list={['bloom', className]}
  data-bloom
  data-act={act}
  data-intensity={intensity}
  aria-hidden="true"
>
  <div class="field">
    <span class="orb a"><i></i></span>
    <span class="orb b"><i></i></span>
    <span class="orb c"><i></i></span>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  .bloom {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* Purely decorative: it must never sit above content or catch a pointer. */
    z-index: 0;
  }

  /*
   * Rest states, set by `src/motion/bloom-rest.ts` on a route that calls it (8.14; the file
   * has the measurements). A field nowhere near the viewport is hidden so its layers stop
   * being drawn; any field is paused while the page scrolls, so its overlap is not blended
   * afresh on every frame of it. Paused rather than removed, so the drift resumes from the
   * pose it left.
   */
  .bloom[data-offscreen] .field,
  .bloom[data-unlit] .field {
    visibility: hidden;
    will-change: auto;
  }

  .bloom[data-offscreen] .orb > i,
  .bloom[data-unlit] .orb > i,
  .bloom[data-scrolling] .orb > i {
    animation-play-state: paused;
  }

  .bloom[data-offscreen] .orb > i,
  .bloom[data-unlit] .orb > i {
    will-change: auto;
  }

  /*
   * `data-unlit` is the third rest state, set by a section's own timeline while the wrapper it
   * places this field in is at opacity 0 (act two before its feed, act four's amber room after
   * its close). The field is invisible either way; hidden, it is also not composited.
   */

  /*
   * Per-act palettes. Three stops each, all from tokens.css - no hex literal appears
   * here, which is what keeps the contrast validator authoritative over the whole site.
   */
  .bloom[data-act='hero'] {
    --bloom-a: var(--color-bloom-magenta);
    --bloom-b: var(--color-plum-500);
    --bloom-c: var(--color-bloom-violet);
  }
  .bloom[data-act='problem'] {
    --bloom-a: var(--color-bloom-violet);
    --bloom-b: var(--color-plum-700);
    --bloom-c: var(--color-plum-950);
  }
  .bloom[data-act='feed'] {
    --bloom-a: var(--color-bloom-cyan);
    --bloom-b: var(--color-bloom-violet);
    --bloom-c: var(--color-plum-800);
  }
  /*
   * Act three. Named `pro` until 2026-09-04, when the owner moved amber from the auto-add
   * act to the notification act - a notification *is* an arrival, which is what amber
   * already meant (`design-system.md` §2). The palette is unchanged; only the act it
   * belongs to is. It had never been used, so nothing rendered differently.
   */
  .bloom[data-act='notify'] {
    --bloom-a: var(--color-bloom-amber);
    --bloom-b: var(--color-plum-600);
    --bloom-c: var(--color-bloom-magenta);
  }

  /*
   * Act four's close - the same three stops as `hero`, deliberately and not by accident.
   * Gate A asks the page to end on the light it opened with, which is a decision worth
   * being able to point at: a separate act name says "these are equal on purpose", where
   * reusing `act="hero"` for the final CTA would leave a future change to the hero's
   * palette silently repainting the close as well.
   */
  .bloom[data-act='cta'] {
    --bloom-a: var(--color-bloom-magenta);
    --bloom-b: var(--color-plum-500);
    --bloom-c: var(--color-bloom-violet);
  }

  .bloom[data-intensity='ambient'] .field {
    opacity: 0.62;
  }
  .bloom[data-intensity='feature'] .field {
    opacity: 0.82;
  }

  /*
   * How far through its section the visitor is, 0–1. Registered so it is typed and has a
   * defined initial value: a route that never sets it still gets a correct static field.
   */
  @property --bloom-scroll {
    syntax: '<number>';
    inherits: true;
    initial-value: 0;
  }

  /*
   * The field carries the scroll response for all three orbs at once - rotating the
   * container turns the whole constellation, which is what reads as the atmosphere
   * *turning* rather than merely sliding. `isolation` keeps the orbs' blending inside the
   * field so it can never lighten the page behind it.
   *
   * Inset -35% rather than -25%: rotating a rectangle by 30 degrees means it must be at
   * least `cos30 + sin30` - about 1.37x - of the viewport in each axis to still cover it,
   * and a tighter oversize lets a hard edge sweep into view mid-turn.
   */
  .field {
    position: absolute;
    inset: -35%;
    isolation: isolate;
    transform: translate3d(0, calc(var(--bloom-scroll) * -11%), 0)
      rotate(calc(var(--bloom-scroll) * 30deg)) scale(calc(1 + var(--bloom-scroll) * 0.24));
    will-change: transform;
  }

  /*
   * Two elements per light, and the split is load-bearing. The wrapper is placed by its
   * *centre* - `left`/`top` name where the light sits and `translate(-50%, -50%)` does the
   * rest - while the inner element owns the orbit. One element cannot do both, because the
   * centring translate and the animated transform are the same property, and the first
   * attempt at this silently lost the centring and put two of the three lights outside the
   * viewport entirely: the field is 1.7x the viewport, so only its middle 21–79% is ever
   * on screen, and percentages read as if they were viewport coordinates are simply wrong.
   */
  .orb {
    position: absolute;
    width: 38%;
    aspect-ratio: 1;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  }

  /*
   * `screen` is the whole reason these read as light. Two overlapping blobs at normal
   * blending give you the nearer one; screened, they add to a brighter, hotter colour
   * exactly where they cross - so the field has moving highlights that no single gradient
   * plate can produce.
   */
  .orb > i {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    border-radius: 50%;
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
    will-change: transform;
  }

  /* Centres in field coordinates, chosen so all three sit in the visible middle band. */
  .orb.a {
    left: 36%;
    top: 38%;
  }
  .orb.a > i {
    background: radial-gradient(closest-side, var(--bloom-a) 0%, transparent 76%);
    animation: orbit-a 25s linear infinite;
  }

  .orb.b {
    width: 33%;
    left: 63%;
    top: 35%;
  }
  .orb.b > i {
    background: radial-gradient(closest-side, var(--bloom-c) 0%, transparent 76%);
    animation: orbit-b 34s linear infinite;
  }

  .orb.c {
    width: 36%;
    left: 49%;
    top: 63%;
  }
  .orb.c > i {
    background: radial-gradient(closest-side, var(--bloom-b) 0%, transparent 76%);
    animation: orbit-c 43s linear infinite;
  }

  /*
   * Three periods that do not divide into each other, two turning one way and one the
   * other. The arrangement therefore does not visibly repeat - the field is never caught
   * returning to a pose the eye has already seen, which is the difference between an
   * animation and something that feels alive.
   *
   * `linear`, deliberately: an eased rotation stalls at the top of every cycle, and once
   * you notice the stall you cannot stop seeing the loop. The swell carries the shape of
   * the motion instead.
   *
   * The orbit radii were roughly halved at Gate C rev 8 and pulled in a further 30% at
   * rev 9 (owner): the lights were carrying far enough out that the composition drifted
   * lopsided and the field stopped reading as one atmosphere. They now stay near the
   * centre and mostly change *how* they overlap rather than where they are, which is the
   * part that was worth having. Periods were slowed 30% at rev 9 for the same reason -
   * the field should be something you notice having changed, not something you watch.
   */
  @keyframes orbit-a {
    0% {
      transform: rotate(0deg) translateX(10%) scale(1);
    }
    50% {
      transform: rotate(180deg) translateX(20%) scale(1.2);
    }
    100% {
      transform: rotate(360deg) translateX(10%) scale(1);
    }
  }
  @keyframes orbit-b {
    0% {
      transform: rotate(0deg) translateX(17%) scale(1.14);
    }
    50% {
      transform: rotate(-180deg) translateX(8%) scale(0.9);
    }
    100% {
      transform: rotate(-360deg) translateX(17%) scale(1.14);
    }
  }
  @keyframes orbit-c {
    0% {
      transform: rotate(0deg) translateX(13%) scale(0.96);
    }
    50% {
      transform: rotate(180deg) translateX(22%) scale(1.26);
    }
    100% {
      transform: rotate(360deg) translateX(13%) scale(0.96);
    }
  }

  /*
   * design-system.md §5: "Bloom drift stops; blooms remain, static." Reduced motion is a
   * designed state, not an absence - the atmosphere is the same, it simply holds still,
   * with the orbs parked in a composed arrangement rather than wherever they happened to
   * be. The scroll response goes too: it is motion the visitor did not ask for.
   */
  /* At `md` and up only since 8.14c round 8 (owner): below it, phones keep the full
   * motion build under Reduce Motion. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) and (min-width: 768px) {
    .field {
      transform: none;
      will-change: auto;
    }

    /*
     * **Each orb by name, not `.orb > i`, and that is a bug fix rather than a style.**
     *
     * `animation: none` sat on `.orb > i` - specificity 0-1-1 - while the animation it was
     * cancelling is declared on `.orb.a > i` at 0-2-1. The override lost, so the blooms
     * went on drifting under reduced motion on every route of the site from 8.1 until
     * 2026-09-08, while the `transform` declarations below (which do match at 0-2-1) were
     * quietly overridden by the running animation. Measured before fixing: **18 running
     * animations on `/` with the media feature matching**.
     *
     * Nothing failed, because the reduced-motion baselines were generated against the
     * defect and then agreed with it - and `freeze()` paused the animations before every
     * snapshot, so the suite never saw them move. Found while making the OG cover generator
     * deterministic, not by a test.
     *
     * `design-system.md` §5 and the appearance approved at 8.1 both say this field is
     * static under reduced motion. `review-gates.md` §4 covers the change: it restores an
     * already-approved appearance rather than proposing a new one.
     */
    .orb.a > i,
    .orb.b > i,
    .orb.c > i {
      animation: none;
      will-change: auto;
    }
    .orb.a > i {
      transform: translateX(30%) scale(1.1);
    }
    .orb.b > i {
      transform: translateX(-18%) scale(1.02);
    }
    .orb.c > i {
      transform: translateX(34%) scale(1.12);
    }
  }
</style>
